That was Steve's interpretation of that script note.

528
00:21:32.700 --> 00:21:34.180
And how funny was that?

529
00:21:34.300 --> 00:21:40.080
You guys, if you read that you scream, I think most of us would just be like, ah, right?

530
00:21:40.320 --> 00:21:40.580
Yes.

531
00:21:40.720 --> 00:21:43.240
But Steve made a meal out of that moment.

532
00:21:43.240 --> 00:21:44.680
In the best way.

533
00:21:44.880 --> 00:21:46.580
He's a comedy genius.

534
00:21:46.580 --> 00:21:47.260
Yes.

535
00:21:47.660 --> 00:21:53.480
You know, Screen Rant did a list of the top 15 pop culture references created by The Office.

536
00:21:53.740 --> 00:21:57.220
And the meme of Steve saying no is one of them.

537
00:21:57.440 --> 00:21:58.460
I don't doubt it.

538
00:21:58.680 --> 00:22:03.740
I have been online where I see people that I don't even know, like respond to other people

539
00:22:03.740 --> 00:22:05.560
with just Steve's face going, no.

540
00:22:06.160 --> 00:22:06.940
It's amazing.

541
00:22:07.460 --> 00:22:10.660
Well, the episode starts and Michael is spiraling.

542
00:22:10.660 --> 00:22:12.020
He's going to call David Wallace.

543
00:22:12.340 --> 00:22:17.600
He's watching stupid Toby as he shows his stupid photos of Costa Rica to Phyllis.

544
00:22:18.080 --> 00:22:18.260
Yeah.

545
00:22:18.520 --> 00:22:22.460
He manages to get David on the phone by texting him 911.

546
00:22:23.400 --> 00:22:24.200
Call me.

547
00:22:24.420 --> 00:22:25.300
But you know what?

548
00:22:25.420 --> 00:22:29.780
Michael said he's learned that people will not return his calls unless he texts 911.

549
00:22:30.220 --> 00:22:33.440
And now everybody does because they think something horrible has happened.

550
00:22:33.440 --> 00:22:36.300
So I had a pager when I first got to L.A.

551
00:22:36.620 --> 00:22:37.480
So did I.

552
00:22:37.680 --> 00:22:41.140
You had to because you didn't know like really have cell phones.

553
00:22:41.280 --> 00:22:42.080
You had a pager.

554
00:22:42.360 --> 00:22:48.320
And the only reason you had a pager was so that your agent could page you if you got an

555
00:22:48.320 --> 00:22:51.040
audition and then you would have to go find a phone.

556
00:22:51.180 --> 00:22:52.320
You'd have to find a pay phone.

557
00:22:52.460 --> 00:22:53.000
Call them back.

558
00:22:53.260 --> 00:22:53.380
Yeah.

559
00:22:53.380 --> 00:22:57.560
I used to keep a Ziploc bag full of change in my car for two reasons.

560
00:22:57.920 --> 00:23:02.920
One, for all of the metered parking that didn't accept credit cards, just coins.

561
00:23:03.280 --> 00:23:09.880
And two, so that I could find a pay phone and call my agent back when my agent paged me,

562
00:23:10.000 --> 00:23:11.720
which was maybe twice a year.

563
00:23:11.880 --> 00:23:12.440
Oh, yeah.

564
00:23:12.580 --> 00:23:17.340
I remember one time distinctly I was driving through Santa Monica with some friends and my

565
00:23:17.340 --> 00:23:24.120
pager went off and I almost swerved off the road because I was like, what's happening?

566
00:23:25.140 --> 00:23:26.420
I've hit the big time.

567
00:23:26.620 --> 00:23:27.460
I've been paged.

568
00:23:27.740 --> 00:23:29.080
We were supposed to be going to the beach.

569
00:23:29.180 --> 00:23:30.260
I was like, no, we can't do that now.

570
00:23:30.320 --> 00:23:31.060
I have to find a pay phone.

571
00:23:31.180 --> 00:23:33.340
So we're all like driving the streets looking for a pay phone.

572
00:23:33.440 --> 00:23:34.040
I pulled over.

573
00:23:34.200 --> 00:23:35.400
I ran up to the pay phone.

574
00:23:35.540 --> 00:23:37.480
I put my quarters in, called them.

575
00:23:37.600 --> 00:23:43.600
And they were like, all right, have you ever needed any kind of glasses?

576
00:23:44.520 --> 00:23:45.340
I'm like, what?

577
00:23:46.340 --> 00:23:46.680
No.

578
00:23:46.880 --> 00:23:49.600
And they're like, we have an audition for you for Lens Crafters.

579
00:23:49.700 --> 00:23:51.240
They would like people who wear glasses.

580
00:23:51.560 --> 00:23:53.000
I'm like, that's not me.

581
00:23:53.000 --> 00:23:53.720
All right.

582
00:23:54.020 --> 00:23:54.220
Bye.

583
00:23:54.600 --> 00:23:55.620
God damn.

584
00:23:56.440 --> 00:23:58.740
Yeah, that's pretty much it.

585
00:23:58.940 --> 00:24:03.620
But then I did have a boyfriend once who was not calling me back.

586
00:24:04.120 --> 00:24:05.740
Did you light up that pager?

587
00:24:06.020 --> 00:24:09.060
I sure did with a bunch of 911s.

588
00:24:09.220 --> 00:24:09.880
You know it.

589
00:24:10.200 --> 00:24:11.480
Did you really, Jenna?

590
00:24:11.700 --> 00:24:11.940
Oh, yeah.

591
00:24:12.180 --> 00:24:13.040
Oh, yeah.

592
00:24:13.320 --> 00:24:14.140
I did.

593
00:24:14.440 --> 00:24:15.900
Were you a psycho girlfriend?

594
00:24:16.600 --> 00:24:17.540
You know what?

595
00:24:17.680 --> 00:24:19.380
He was cheating on me that night.

596
00:24:19.520 --> 00:24:20.940
Oh, so you knew.

597
00:24:21.140 --> 00:24:21.820
You knew.

598
00:24:21.820 --> 00:24:23.100
I knew it.

599
00:24:23.240 --> 00:24:24.700
We broke up after that.

600
00:24:25.260 --> 00:24:26.380
Oh, man.

601
00:24:26.780 --> 00:24:27.060
Yeah.

602
00:24:27.320 --> 00:24:28.620
I wouldn't light up the pager.

603
00:24:28.880 --> 00:24:30.380
I'm like sitting here calling you psycho.

604
00:24:30.580 --> 00:24:34.340
I wouldn't light up the pager, but I would drive over and like hide out in a bush.

605
00:24:35.020 --> 00:24:38.360
That's so much more elaborate than texting 911.

606
00:24:38.880 --> 00:24:40.100
Here was my plan.

607
00:24:40.100 --> 00:24:42.060
I was like, I know he's cheating on me.

608
00:24:42.140 --> 00:24:47.140
So I'm going to light up that pager and make it buzz so that while they're whatever they're

609
00:24:47.140 --> 00:24:50.820
doing, the pager's just like zing, zing, zing.

610
00:24:50.900 --> 00:24:51.340
Yeah.

611
00:24:51.640 --> 00:24:53.620
I'm just going to ruin their good time.

612
00:24:53.820 --> 00:24:55.360
He probably just turned it off.

613
00:24:55.360 --> 00:24:59.080
I guess I'm just going to jump out of a bush and be like, hey, what are you doing?

614
00:24:59.260 --> 00:24:59.520
Gotcha.

615
00:25:01.220 --> 00:25:05.520
Now I'm going to go home because I've got twigs in my hair and I'm embarrassed.

616
00:25:05.840 --> 00:25:08.040
Is this a plot line for mom detectives?

617
00:25:08.380 --> 00:25:12.960
Should we set mom detectives in the past where all we have are pagers and bush hiding?

618
00:25:13.200 --> 00:25:13.560
Yes.

619
00:25:14.180 --> 00:25:15.760
Oh, bush hiding.

620
00:25:16.700 --> 00:25:17.360
Uh-oh.

621
00:25:17.360 --> 00:25:20.080
So that could be something else.

622
00:25:20.440 --> 00:25:21.520
What's wrong with this?

623
00:25:21.760 --> 00:25:23.020
Can we call it bush hiders?

624
00:25:23.380 --> 00:25:23.820
No.

625
00:25:25.860 --> 00:25:26.740
We can't.

626
00:25:26.820 --> 00:25:28.260
It doesn't even make any sense.

627
00:25:28.700 --> 00:25:30.260
It doesn't make any sense.

628
00:25:30.580 --> 00:25:31.380
You go pitch it.

629
00:25:31.480 --> 00:25:32.240
Hi, our show.

630
00:25:32.460 --> 00:25:33.580
It's called bush hiders.

631
00:25:33.780 --> 00:25:37.480
And if you don't want to buy it, you're an a**hole because this is gold.

632
00:25:37.840 --> 00:25:39.200
And they're like, what's it about?

633
00:25:39.320 --> 00:25:41.360
And we're like, it's two mom detectives.

634
00:25:41.600 --> 00:25:44.320
It's two older women in comfortable clothing.

635
00:25:44.700 --> 00:25:45.000
Yeah.

636
00:25:45.100 --> 00:25:46.740
We're not wearing any designer clothes.

637
00:25:46.740 --> 00:25:49.140
We want like 30-minute wardrobe sessions.

638
00:25:49.440 --> 00:25:50.540
It won't be fitted.

639
00:25:51.900 --> 00:25:56.260
And we want a lot of old tech that no one watching it will really understand.

640
00:25:56.580 --> 00:25:58.060
And we're going to hide in some bushes.

641
00:25:58.620 --> 00:25:58.820
Yeah.

642
00:25:59.400 --> 00:26:00.060
Do you want it?

643
00:26:03.740 --> 00:26:05.200
Okay, where are we?

644
00:26:05.300 --> 00:26:08.020
We shouldn't have shared that on the air because someone's going to steal it.

645
00:26:08.080 --> 00:26:13.020
I guarantee you in two years, you're going to turn on HBO Max and there's going to be

646
00:26:13.020 --> 00:26:15.740
two 20-year-olds starring in a show called Bush Hiders.

647
00:26:15.740 --> 00:26:17.000
Mark my word.

648
00:26:17.940 --> 00:26:18.760
Mark my word.

649
00:26:18.900 --> 00:26:21.520
And they're going to be young, hot detectives in designer clothes.

650
00:26:24.280 --> 00:26:25.920
That's how it works in this business.

651
00:26:26.240 --> 00:26:26.920
That's right.

652
00:26:27.360 --> 00:26:28.420
I'm not jaded.

653
00:26:29.060 --> 00:26:29.680
All right.

654
00:26:30.300 --> 00:26:31.320
Should we move on?

655
00:26:31.440 --> 00:26:32.520
What's happened to me in New York?

656
00:26:32.640 --> 00:26:33.600
Who am I here?

657
00:26:33.760 --> 00:26:34.540
I don't know.

658
00:26:34.760 --> 00:26:38.560
You just took a big swig of coffee and you're like, that's how it works in the business.

659
00:26:39.060 --> 00:26:39.820
Well, listen.

660
00:26:40.100 --> 00:26:40.520
You know what?

661
00:26:40.520 --> 00:26:42.840
David Wallace tells Michael he's just going to have to deal with it.

662
00:26:43.020 --> 00:26:43.220
Yeah.

663
00:26:43.300 --> 00:26:47.200
And he couldn't believe that one of his employees had been back so long and he didn't know.

664
00:26:47.460 --> 00:26:49.960
And Dwight and Michael let him know that the annex is icky.

665
00:26:50.180 --> 00:26:51.060
It's icky back there.

666
00:26:51.620 --> 00:26:51.840
Yeah.

667
00:26:51.840 --> 00:26:55.500
And he really wants David to get rid of Toby.

668
00:26:55.760 --> 00:26:58.820
And David says, I can't get rid of him without cause.

669
00:26:59.100 --> 00:27:01.700
And Michael says, he has cause.

670
00:27:01.880 --> 00:27:03.880
It's because I hate him.

671
00:27:04.140 --> 00:27:04.740
Because?

672
00:27:05.240 --> 00:27:07.840
But that is not obviously cause.

673
00:27:08.080 --> 00:27:09.360
Well, they're going to find something.

674
00:27:09.520 --> 00:27:11.980
But Jenna, another issue is happening.

675
00:27:12.340 --> 00:27:12.520
Yeah.

676
00:27:12.680 --> 00:27:14.640
It's a day of drama in the office.

677
00:27:14.920 --> 00:27:20.160
Pam is preparing food in the kitchen and she discovers a mess in the microwave.

678
00:27:20.160 --> 00:27:21.000
Jenna?

679
00:27:21.420 --> 00:27:21.560
Yeah.

680
00:27:21.680 --> 00:27:23.220
Did you see what Pam was preparing?

681
00:27:23.500 --> 00:27:25.320
I know what Pam was preparing.

682
00:27:26.040 --> 00:27:26.460
Share.

683
00:27:26.900 --> 00:27:32.740
This is maybe one of my most frequently asked questions outside of what was in the teapot note.

684
00:27:33.080 --> 00:27:34.040
No way.

685
00:27:34.240 --> 00:27:34.560
Yes.

686
00:27:34.760 --> 00:27:39.860
People are like, what is Pam microwaving in the episode with the dirty microwave?

687
00:27:40.180 --> 00:27:41.420
Here are some guesses.

688
00:27:41.760 --> 00:27:42.460
We got mail.

689
00:27:42.860 --> 00:27:45.680
Here are what people are guessing that I was microwaving.

690
00:27:46.100 --> 00:27:46.320
Okay.

691
00:27:46.780 --> 00:27:47.620
Apple juice.

692
00:27:47.780 --> 00:27:47.980
Hmm.

693
00:27:48.340 --> 00:27:49.820
A probiotic drink.

694
00:27:50.160 --> 00:27:50.720
Okay.

695
00:27:51.080 --> 00:27:51.800
Egg whites.

696
00:27:52.280 --> 00:27:53.340
Bone broth.

697
00:27:53.560 --> 00:27:53.780
Hmm.

698
00:27:54.240 --> 00:27:54.820
Soup.

699
00:27:55.300 --> 00:27:55.880
Milk.

700
00:27:56.420 --> 00:27:57.020
Porridge.

701
00:27:57.300 --> 00:27:58.140
And yogurt.

702
00:27:58.480 --> 00:27:59.960
Well, I had no idea.

703
00:27:59.960 --> 00:28:01.860
So I looked at the script.

704
00:28:02.100 --> 00:28:03.280
But Jenna, you tell us.

705
00:28:03.660 --> 00:28:04.720
It's egg whites.

706
00:28:04.940 --> 00:28:06.000
It's egg whites.

707
00:28:06.280 --> 00:28:10.320
Which I, by the way, knowing my BFF in real life, thought was kind of fitting.

708
00:28:10.820 --> 00:28:11.360
Yeah.

709
00:28:11.560 --> 00:28:14.560
Because you can buy egg whites in these little cups.

710
00:28:14.860 --> 00:28:16.340
And that's what she's doing.

711
00:28:16.340 --> 00:28:18.400
She's going to microwave herself some egg whites.

712
00:28:18.820 --> 00:28:22.320
A lot of people also asked, what was in the microwave?

713
00:28:22.800 --> 00:28:24.440
That is also in the script.

714
00:28:24.680 --> 00:28:25.100
Mm-hmm.

715
00:28:25.240 --> 00:28:28.540
And it just says, like, a saucy mess, basically.

716
00:28:28.900 --> 00:28:30.020
I typed it out.

717
00:28:30.020 --> 00:28:35.640
It says, Pam opens the microwave door to put in some egg whites, but the microwave is splattered

718
00:28:35.640 --> 00:28:37.420
all over with red sauce.

719
00:28:37.760 --> 00:28:37.920
Yeah.

720
00:28:38.320 --> 00:28:39.880
Now we're in the break room.

721
00:28:40.240 --> 00:28:42.000
And what would we title this?

722
00:28:43.040 --> 00:28:45.060
Jim's exposed in the break room?

723
00:28:45.160 --> 00:28:46.160
No, that doesn't sound good.

724
00:28:46.160 --> 00:28:47.340
Exposure in the break room.

725
00:28:47.660 --> 00:28:49.000
Exposure in the break room.

726
00:28:49.260 --> 00:28:51.700
Andy, I guess, has peepers like a hawk.

727
00:28:51.820 --> 00:28:56.600
And read Jim's email and found out that Jim is buying his parents' house and hasn't

728
00:28:56.600 --> 00:28:57.220
told Pam.

729
00:28:57.540 --> 00:28:57.880
Yeah.

730
00:28:58.160 --> 00:29:01.340
Or as Kevin puts it, the same house he used to pee the bed in.

731
00:29:01.780 --> 00:29:03.060
Phyllis thinks it's romantic.

732
00:29:03.440 --> 00:29:05.560
Until she asks Jim where it is.

733
00:29:05.920 --> 00:29:07.460
Jim says it's on Linden Ave.

734
00:29:07.780 --> 00:29:07.980
Mm-hmm.

735
00:29:07.980 --> 00:29:09.420
And Phyllis makes a face.

736
00:29:09.540 --> 00:29:12.880
And then in the background, you see me kind of roll my eyes like, ooh.

737
00:29:13.520 --> 00:29:15.660
I looked up Linden Ave in Scranton.

738
00:29:15.780 --> 00:29:17.020
I couldn't find a Linden Ave.

739
00:29:17.140 --> 00:29:20.760
I found a Linden Street, but it's sort of like in the heart of the city.

740
00:29:20.900 --> 00:29:24.640
There's like shops and churches and definitely no quarry nearby.

741
00:29:25.320 --> 00:29:25.580
Oh.

742
00:29:26.100 --> 00:29:26.260
Hmm.

743
00:29:26.820 --> 00:29:27.280
Interesting.

744
00:29:27.560 --> 00:29:31.620
We got a lot of questions from, for example, Brittany Y.

745
00:29:31.920 --> 00:29:32.540
and Jenner B.

746
00:29:32.920 --> 00:29:35.680
Whose house did they use for Jim's parents' house?

747
00:29:35.880 --> 00:29:41.140
Was the interior a set or was it the actual house that they used for the exterior?

748
00:29:41.140 --> 00:29:44.720
This is where Randy Cordray really came through for me, Ange.

749
00:29:44.920 --> 00:29:46.080
Bring it home, Randy.

750
00:29:46.360 --> 00:29:49.820
I remembered that it was two different houses.

751
00:29:49.820 --> 00:29:53.800
We used one for the interior and one for the exterior.

752
00:29:54.160 --> 00:30:00.900
And Randy said that our location manager, Kyle Alexander, had found several choices and that

753
00:30:00.900 --> 00:30:06.300
Randy had really hoped that they could find a single residence that they could do both

754
00:30:06.300 --> 00:30:11.740
interior and exterior just because it would be so much easier to shoot at just one location.

755
00:30:11.800 --> 00:30:13.600
But it didn't work out that way.

756
00:30:13.980 --> 00:30:18.600
Randy said everything they looked at either worked for the interior but not the exterior

757
00:30:18.600 --> 00:30:19.560
or vice versa.

758
00:30:19.960 --> 00:30:27.240
So for the exterior, we used an occupied home on Calvert Street in Van Nuys, California.

759
00:30:27.800 --> 00:30:31.640
But the interior was not suitable to shoot.

760
00:30:31.940 --> 00:30:35.480
This was because it was absolutely adorable inside.

761
00:30:35.780 --> 00:30:37.540
And it needed to look a little janky.

762
00:30:37.820 --> 00:30:38.180
Yeah.

763
00:30:38.200 --> 00:30:39.320
It was so cute.

764
00:30:39.520 --> 00:30:44.800
And when we were shooting there, they would let John and I step in to the house to kind

765
00:30:44.800 --> 00:30:46.740
of get our makeup refreshed and touched up.

766
00:30:46.860 --> 00:30:51.820
But we would just stand right inside that front door and then we would go back outside again.

767
00:30:51.940 --> 00:30:52.980
And that was it.

768
00:30:53.160 --> 00:30:54.380
And it was cute inside?

769
00:30:54.660 --> 00:30:55.140
Oh, my God.

770
00:30:55.140 --> 00:30:56.060
It was so cute.

771
00:30:56.060 --> 00:31:02.020
It was decorated like, oh, like an English professor's home.

772
00:31:02.580 --> 00:31:12.160
Like just cozy, cozy, literary bookshelves, just beautiful kind of restored home inside.

773
00:31:12.200 --> 00:31:13.040
So great.

774
00:31:13.640 --> 00:31:16.940
I did something similar one time on this show called Haters Back Off.

775
00:31:17.060 --> 00:31:19.660
We filmed the exterior in one house and the interior in another.

776
00:31:19.960 --> 00:31:23.880
And when we were filming the exterior, it was cold and kind of misty raining.

777
00:31:23.880 --> 00:31:26.940
And they let us step inside just the front room.

778
00:31:27.040 --> 00:31:27.620
Yeah, same.

779
00:31:27.780 --> 00:31:28.540
When we waited, right?

780
00:31:28.540 --> 00:31:28.740
Yeah.

781
00:31:29.020 --> 00:31:30.560
We didn't go throughout their house at all.

782
00:31:30.720 --> 00:31:34.740
But we stepped in the front room and I was with Steve Little and we weren't really sure

783
00:31:34.740 --> 00:31:35.320
what to expect.

784
00:31:35.320 --> 00:31:40.800
We opened the door to step in and there was maybe 4,000 tiny dolls.

785
00:31:41.340 --> 00:31:41.680
What?

786
00:31:42.040 --> 00:31:42.480
Yes.

787
00:31:42.720 --> 00:31:43.040
What?

788
00:31:43.040 --> 00:31:47.880
There were dolls and cases, all kinds of fancy dolls in every kind of case you could

