It was the next day.

1544
01:03:52.750 --> 01:04:00.290
But in editing, they moved it up, and we are going to end instead with a delicious Dwight

1545
01:04:00.290 --> 01:04:01.050
talking head.

1546
01:04:01.250 --> 01:04:02.110
We'll get to it.

1547
01:04:02.250 --> 01:04:07.130
But first, Jim needs to show Pam what he's done.

1548
01:04:07.690 --> 01:04:08.130
That's right.

1549
01:04:08.490 --> 01:04:09.830
Jim is nervous but excited.

1550
01:04:10.070 --> 01:04:12.730
He is, as my daughter says, nervous-sided.

1551
01:04:13.470 --> 01:04:16.310
And she's like, why are we stopping at your parents' house?

1552
01:04:16.490 --> 01:04:18.950
And he says, I bought it.

1553
01:04:18.950 --> 01:04:23.590
And then, Jenna, at 18 minutes, two seconds, Jim says, let's go inside.

1554
01:04:23.590 --> 01:04:24.590
I'll show you inside.

1555
01:04:24.990 --> 01:04:25.430
What?

1556
01:04:25.790 --> 01:04:27.730
Has Pam never been inside the house?

1557
01:04:28.050 --> 01:04:29.570
We got so much mail about that.

1558
01:04:29.890 --> 01:04:30.730
People wrote in.

1559
01:04:30.910 --> 01:04:34.710
They said, if Pam knew they were at Jim's parents' house when they pulled up, wouldn't that mean

1560
01:04:34.710 --> 01:04:37.110
she's been there before and she's probably been inside?

1561
01:04:37.450 --> 01:04:39.930
You would hope Pam has been inside his parents' house.

1562
01:04:40.030 --> 01:04:40.990
They've been dating for a while.

1563
01:04:41.070 --> 01:04:42.130
They live in the same town.

1564
01:04:42.450 --> 01:04:46.790
Yeah, and it's interesting because his parents have moved out, but they've left some furniture.

1565
01:04:46.790 --> 01:04:49.670
Also, how rough were they on this house?

1566
01:04:49.970 --> 01:04:50.670
I know.

1567
01:04:51.030 --> 01:04:54.070
And Jim says, I was helping my mom out.

1568
01:04:54.390 --> 01:04:55.390
Where's his dad?

1569
01:04:55.630 --> 01:04:56.550
I don't know.

1570
01:04:56.710 --> 01:04:57.830
I have a lot of questions.

1571
01:04:58.070 --> 01:04:59.670
There were a lot of questions.

1572
01:05:00.090 --> 01:05:00.910
Rachel M.

1573
01:05:01.070 --> 01:05:01.910
and Megan L.

1574
01:05:02.270 --> 01:05:03.850
said, question for Jenna.

1575
01:05:04.090 --> 01:05:09.170
In this episode, when Jim is showing Pam his parents' house, I was wondering what went

1576
01:05:09.170 --> 01:05:11.690
into how you decided to play this scene?

1577
01:05:11.690 --> 01:05:18.170
Because she seems really worried and unexcited, but then in the end, she says she loves it.

1578
01:05:18.530 --> 01:05:23.190
Any input you could give about how you decided to play this or what the director told you

1579
01:05:23.190 --> 01:05:24.770
would be super appreciated.

1580
01:05:25.090 --> 01:05:31.450
Well, guys, we've already discussed my personal opinions on someone buying someone else a house

1581
01:05:31.450 --> 01:05:32.510
without telling them.

1582
01:05:32.510 --> 01:05:40.150
So this was a little bit of a mental gymnastics for me to get Pam to the place where she's

1583
01:05:40.150 --> 01:05:43.890
going to say she loves it and where she says that line.

1584
01:05:44.070 --> 01:05:51.810
I mean, you bought me a house because it was like not how I would react as Jenna.

1585
01:05:52.050 --> 01:05:56.790
You know, I had to really play the character and play the character's truth.

1586
01:05:56.790 --> 01:06:03.670
And in the script, I was being told that my character's truth was that she loved this gesture

1587
01:06:03.670 --> 01:06:05.610
and it made her feel loved.

1588
01:06:06.210 --> 01:06:06.370
Yeah.

1589
01:06:06.670 --> 01:06:08.350
Well, I mean, I think we often did that.

1590
01:06:08.510 --> 01:06:10.430
Obviously, we had to play the truth for the character.

1591
01:06:10.790 --> 01:06:15.890
I mean, I hope you guys know I'm not very much like Angela Martin and how I would react

1592
01:06:15.890 --> 01:06:21.490
to things, but it made sense to me, Jenna for Pam, you know, and Jim made the garage

1593
01:06:21.490 --> 01:06:25.970
into this art studio and he's really putting down some roots with her.

1594
01:06:25.970 --> 01:06:32.790
And I think Pam wanted a family and she wanted all of these things that Jim is offering her.

1595
01:06:33.010 --> 01:06:34.310
She had wanted that.

1596
01:06:34.870 --> 01:06:35.010
Yeah.

1597
01:06:35.190 --> 01:06:41.310
You know, I had thought a little bit back on The Talking Head where Pam discusses what

1598
01:06:41.310 --> 01:06:45.370
her perfect home would look like, how she always wanted a home with a terrace.

1599
01:06:45.890 --> 01:06:46.330
Mm-hmm.

1600
01:06:46.830 --> 01:06:51.610
You know, picking her home seemed like something that Pam was attached to.

1601
01:06:51.750 --> 01:06:53.510
So I did bring that up.

1602
01:06:53.510 --> 01:07:00.110
I said, you know, we've established that Pam has daydreamed about what her home would look

1603
01:07:00.110 --> 01:07:00.430
like.

1604
01:07:00.610 --> 01:07:04.130
And now this person has taken her out of the process.

1605
01:07:04.490 --> 01:07:07.870
You brought that up to the writers at the time of when we were filming?

1606
01:07:08.250 --> 01:07:08.450
Yeah.

1607
01:07:08.750 --> 01:07:16.490
And so I was saying, like, help me understand why Pam now is so happy and so excited.

1608
01:07:16.490 --> 01:07:23.230
And Angela, we got to exactly what you just said, which was that it was a vulnerable thing

1609
01:07:23.230 --> 01:07:26.110
for Pam to give up the art school track.

1610
01:07:26.350 --> 01:07:29.890
And she did that because of two reasons.

1611
01:07:30.310 --> 01:07:35.750
One, graphic design wasn't what she meant she wanted to do as an artiSt.

1612
01:07:35.750 --> 01:07:38.110
And two, she's ready.

1613
01:07:38.390 --> 01:07:45.790
She's very, very ready to start living this married life, to be in the season of family

1614
01:07:45.790 --> 01:07:50.650
and marriage with this man that she's been waiting years for.

1615
01:07:50.930 --> 01:07:57.010
And so this home is her fast track to that season of her life.

1616
01:07:57.150 --> 01:07:58.230
And she's just ready.

1617
01:07:59.150 --> 01:08:01.750
And, you know, they're going to get to fix it up together.

1618
01:08:01.750 --> 01:08:05.310
So there's still that element of nesting that's going to get to happen.

1619
01:08:05.570 --> 01:08:10.590
And so that's kind of what I was thinking was like, I'm ready.

1620
01:08:10.930 --> 01:08:11.310
Yeah.

1621
01:08:11.490 --> 01:08:17.590
And you have to think, when Pam talked about her dream house with this tower and the terrace

1622
01:08:17.590 --> 01:08:20.970
and all of that, she was in an unhappy relationship.

1623
01:08:21.130 --> 01:08:25.330
When you're in a place you feel stuck in life or you're unhappy, you daydream.

1624
01:08:25.330 --> 01:08:30.310
And you daydream about these fantastical things and their coping mechanisMs.

1625
01:08:30.850 --> 01:08:35.910
And then when you're with someone you really love, you don't need the fantasy anymore.

1626
01:08:36.370 --> 01:08:38.030
That's such a good point, Angela.

1627
01:08:38.290 --> 01:08:39.690
You're just ready to have a life.

1628
01:08:39.850 --> 01:08:41.790
And she's ready to have that life.

1629
01:08:42.070 --> 01:08:42.410
Yeah.

1630
01:08:42.770 --> 01:08:45.130
Now, we did get some mail about that garage.

1631
01:08:45.930 --> 01:08:46.510
Katie C.

1632
01:08:46.670 --> 01:08:47.370
and Chris G.

1633
01:08:47.610 --> 01:08:47.850
said,

1634
01:08:47.990 --> 01:08:52.450
When Jim is showing Pam his parents' house, he says the garage has great lighting.

1635
01:08:52.450 --> 01:08:58.850
He then proceeds to open a windowless garage door, revealing a completely windowless garage space.

1636
01:08:59.170 --> 01:09:00.730
Where's the great lighting, Jim?

1637
01:09:00.990 --> 01:09:01.470
Where?

1638
01:09:01.890 --> 01:09:02.350
Okay.

1639
01:09:02.530 --> 01:09:03.770
Well, I thought that was really funny.

1640
01:09:04.190 --> 01:09:05.370
That is really funny.

1641
01:09:05.530 --> 01:09:10.270
I want you guys to know that I have a garage that we use as a home office.

1642
01:09:10.710 --> 01:09:14.070
And for a long time, we would just raise the door.

1643
01:09:14.250 --> 01:09:16.970
And then there would be amazing light, you know?

1644
01:09:17.550 --> 01:09:21.050
And so it did get great light, but you had to raise the door.

1645
01:09:21.330 --> 01:09:25.410
And then years later, we actually just removed the door because we're like,

1646
01:09:25.470 --> 01:09:27.230
this is never going to be a garage again.

1647
01:09:27.450 --> 01:09:32.150
You know, we've sort of made it into this little home office and put an actual sliding glass door.

1648
01:09:32.470 --> 01:09:33.970
But I get it.

1649
01:09:33.970 --> 01:09:35.290
It does have great lighting.

1650
01:09:35.390 --> 01:09:36.710
You just have to open it up.

1651
01:09:36.710 --> 01:09:45.110
But he does present it like this garage has great lighting, as if the other garages that you open,

1652
01:09:45.250 --> 01:09:48.290
the whole side of the building, wouldn't have great lighting.

1653
01:09:48.410 --> 01:09:53.710
He's presenting as if it's like a special feature that kind of applies to every garage

1654
01:09:53.710 --> 01:09:58.430
that doesn't have like a giant tree planted in front of it, which it wouldn't because it's a garage.

1655
01:09:58.690 --> 01:09:58.930
Right.

1656
01:09:59.090 --> 01:10:01.470
Most garages are just boxes with no windows.

1657
01:10:02.190 --> 01:10:05.690
I have to tell you two things about shooting these scenes at the house.

1658
01:10:06.710 --> 01:10:14.830
Scene where Jim is trying to take the clown painting off the wall for Pam is one of the hardest I've ever laughed on the show.

1659
01:10:14.990 --> 01:10:16.710
It is the second hardest time.

1660
01:10:16.910 --> 01:10:22.670
The first was when we did the scene in Dinner Party where Michael's showing us the tiny TV on the wall.

1661
01:10:23.650 --> 01:10:24.830
This is the second.

1662
01:10:25.770 --> 01:10:31.330
Every time John tried to lift the painting on the wall, I could not stop laughing.

1663
01:10:31.330 --> 01:10:32.830
I don't know what it was.

1664
01:10:33.110 --> 01:10:36.150
Tears, tears, choking on our laughter.

1665
01:10:36.530 --> 01:10:37.790
We laughed so hard.

1666
01:10:37.990 --> 01:10:41.010
I looked for it in the bloopers and it's not there.

1667
01:10:41.130 --> 01:10:42.830
And I'm so upset it's not there.

1668
01:10:42.970 --> 01:10:50.270
It took us like an hour to shoot that tiny moment of him trying to take the painting off the wall.

1669
01:10:50.490 --> 01:10:53.270
John is also so funny at physical comedy.

1670
01:10:53.650 --> 01:10:54.090
Yes.

1671
01:10:54.090 --> 01:10:58.770
I laughed out loud when he peeled back that fake wood paneling and it kind of startled him.

1672
01:10:59.210 --> 01:10:59.430
Yes.

1673
01:10:59.670 --> 01:11:07.770
So then at the end of the scene in the garage, when we're hugging, I improvised the line, what about the clown?

1674
01:11:08.250 --> 01:11:09.730
And it made John laugh.

1675
01:11:09.850 --> 01:11:12.910
And that's why he's like burying his head into my neck.

1676
01:11:13.130 --> 01:11:18.470
And then when he said, yeah, I can't do anything about that, it made me laugh.

1677
01:11:18.470 --> 01:11:21.150
And so I'm like smushing my face into his shoulder.

1678
01:11:21.150 --> 01:11:22.810
You can totally tell we're breaking.

1679
01:11:23.150 --> 01:11:29.090
And that's why it was because we were still, anytime we brought up the clown painting, we lost it.

1680
01:11:29.690 --> 01:11:30.870
I love that moment.

1681
01:11:31.030 --> 01:11:33.350
It made it intimate and funny and sweet.

1682
01:11:33.670 --> 01:11:37.510
I think it's time to talk about the greatest Dwight talking head of all time.

1683
01:11:37.790 --> 01:11:44.990
That's saying a lot because there are some amazing Dwight talking heads, but this one really is like top five.

1684
01:11:45.230 --> 01:11:47.310
He's going to describe his perfect crime.

1685
01:11:47.590 --> 01:11:49.310
And I think we have to hear it.

1686
01:11:49.930 --> 01:11:51.150
What is my perfect crime?

1687
01:11:51.750 --> 01:11:53.370
I break into Tiffany's at midnight.

1688
01:11:53.930 --> 01:11:55.050
Do I go for the vault?

1689
01:11:55.230 --> 01:11:56.710
No, I go for the chandelier.

1690
01:11:56.890 --> 01:11:57.470
It's priceless.

1691
01:11:57.990 --> 01:12:00.130
As I'm taking it down, a woman catches me.

1692
01:12:00.350 --> 01:12:01.350
She tells me to stop.

1693
01:12:01.570 --> 01:12:02.710
It's her father's business.

1694
01:12:02.950 --> 01:12:03.550
She's Tiffany.

1695
01:12:03.810 --> 01:12:04.530
I say no.

1696
01:12:04.770 --> 01:12:05.850
We make love all night.

1697
01:12:06.070 --> 01:12:08.950
In the morning, the cops come and I escape in one of their uniforMs.

1698
01:12:09.210 --> 01:12:11.850
I tell her to meet me in Mexico, but I go to Canada.

1699
01:12:12.050 --> 01:12:12.910
I don't trust her.

1700
01:12:13.330 --> 01:12:14.650
Besides, I like the cold.

1701
01:12:15.370 --> 01:12:17.030
Thirty years later, I get a postcard.

1702
01:12:17.210 --> 01:12:19.410
I have a son, and he's the chief of police.

1703
01:12:19.650 --> 01:12:21.130
This is where the story gets interesting.

1704
01:12:21.530 --> 01:12:24.590
I tell Tiffany to meet me in Paris by the Trocadero.

1705
01:12:25.490 --> 01:12:27.110
She's been waiting for me all these years.

1706
01:12:27.250 --> 01:12:28.350
She's never taken another lover.

1707
01:12:28.570 --> 01:12:29.350
I don't care.

1708
01:12:29.510 --> 01:12:30.130
I don't show up.

1709
01:12:30.130 --> 01:12:30.850
I go to Berlin.

1710
01:12:31.070 --> 01:12:32.970
That's where I stashed the chandelier.

1711
01:12:34.030 --> 01:12:34.590
Yeah.

1712
01:12:34.810 --> 01:12:40.490
I do not know how Rain got through saying, this is where the story gets interesting.

1713
01:12:41.170 --> 01:12:42.090
I know.

1714
01:12:42.790 --> 01:12:45.930
Every time I watched this episode, I laughed at that line.

1715
01:12:46.090 --> 01:12:47.430
And I watched it like three times.

1716
01:12:47.630 --> 01:12:52.250
This entire talking head was scripted, and it was delivered as scripted.

1717
01:12:52.410 --> 01:12:56.050
I think this is one of the most brilliantly written pieces ever.

1718
01:12:56.410 --> 01:12:57.990
It's just so good.

1719
01:12:57.990 --> 01:12:59.330
It's so good.

1720
01:12:59.330 --> 01:13:02.510
And who better to deliver it than Rainn Wilson as Dwight?

1721
01:13:02.670 --> 01:13:04.130
It was just perfect.

1722
01:13:04.490 --> 01:13:05.230
I agree.

1723
01:13:05.610 --> 01:13:07.030
There you have it, everyone.

1724
01:13:07.230 --> 01:13:08.110
That's frame Toby.

1725
01:13:08.450 --> 01:13:09.630
Did you have anything else, Angela?

1726
01:13:09.930 --> 01:13:11.490
I don't have anything else.

1727
01:13:11.490 --> 01:13:14.770
I do want you guys to know, because Jen and I both watched the bloopers,

1728
01:13:15.310 --> 01:13:19.910
next week on The Surplus was one of the hardest I've ever laughed in a scene.

1729
01:13:20.170 --> 01:13:21.210
Oh, I can't wait.

1730
01:13:21.830 --> 01:13:27.590
Thank you so much to Randy Cordray, James Carey, and Jen Salata and Paul Lieberstein.

1731
01:13:27.590 --> 01:13:29.270
I traded some emails with them as well.

1732
01:13:29.750 --> 01:13:30.950
Man, that was fun.

1733
01:13:31.150 --> 01:13:32.350
That was a delight.

1734
01:13:32.610 --> 01:13:36.290
I will be trying to clean my microwave using your method.

1735
01:13:36.650 --> 01:13:37.510
Thank you.

1736
01:13:38.250 --> 01:13:40.930
And we'll see you here next week.

1737
01:13:41.090 --> 01:13:42.790
I'll be back in LA next week.

1738
01:13:42.910 --> 01:13:45.710
I wonder, will I bring New York Jenna home with me?

1739
01:13:45.850 --> 01:13:46.210
We'll see.

1740
01:13:46.810 --> 01:13:47.330
I don't know.

1741
01:13:47.430 --> 01:13:50.150
New York Jenna might get the E next to our podcaSt.

1742
01:13:50.710 --> 01:13:51.190
Sorry.

1743
01:13:52.150 --> 01:13:53.130
What can I say?

1744
01:13:54.730 --> 01:13:56.070
See you next week, guys.

1745
01:13:56.310 --> 01:13:57.250
See you next week.

1746
01:14:00.420 --> 01:14:02.940
Thank you for listening to Office Ladies' Second Drink.

